# crawl-body-not-an-assertion.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M137c): a step in a `crawl` body that is not
# one of the three `violations` assertions — demonstrates `tflw check`'s TF070 (checker, tflw M137c /
# D443/D450/D463, the pentest arc's Tier 4).
#
# **What this rule protects is the claim that made Tier 4 a declaration instead of a sixth workload
# kind**: a crawl is a *source of requests*, not a kind of judgement. It issues one request per
# discovered route per declared principal, and each `expect` in its body judges every one of those
# responses. So an `api` step there is a request nobody will send under a principal nobody chose, and
# `expect status equals 200` names a response the construct does not have — because a crawl has many.
# One repair covers the whole family, which is what earns one code: put the step in a `test`.
#
# **Enforced by the checker, not the grammar**, and `ast.ts`'s `CrawlDecl.body` says so. The parser
# admits any step on purpose, so that a misplaced one gets this sentence rather than `expected an
# expect` — the same layering `retry` (D96) and the browser-step rejection (D19) already use.
#
# **Deliberately not `TF033`.** That is what D19's sibling rule reuses, and it is the counter-example
# rather than the pattern: `TF033` predates the one-code-one-repair rule this arc settled on and
# already carries several unrelated repairs.
#
# **`TF069` does not exist and never will.** tflw's D456 withdrew it, and by the time this code was
# minted six comments across three packages already used that number as a pointer to the withdrawal —
# so it was spent without ever being allocated (D463). A gap in the sequence here is a decision.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/crawl-body-not-an-assertion.tflw

crawl "a crawl that tries to write its own requests"
  seed traffic
  # The headline case, and the one D450 leads with. Under which principal would this be sent?
  api GET /products
  expect response has no critical security violations

crawl "a crawl that asserts about one response"
  seed traffic
  # Both of these name `response` in the singular. A crawl has as many responses as it discovered
  # routes times declared principals, so there is no one status to equal and no one header to read.
  expect status equals 200
  expect header "content-type" contains "json"

crawl "a crawl that binds values"
  seed traffic
  # Nothing downstream in a crawl body could read either of these — there is no step order to carry a
  # value along, only assertions applied to every response.
  let wanted = "shoes"
  capture body.id as firstProductId

# --- the negative control: the three families the body does accept -------------------------------
#
# All three, in both the fatal and the soft form. The rule turns on the **matcher**, not on whether
# the step is fatal, so `check` is accepted for the same reason `expect` is — and asserting that here
# is what stops a future tightening from quietly refusing the soft half.

crawl "everything a crawl body may contain" as shopper, peer
  seed openapi "/openapi.json"
  seed traffic
  exclude "/internal/**"
  expect response has no critical security violations
  expect response has no critical authorization violations
  expect response has no input handling violations
  check response has no serious security violations
  check response has no critical authorization violations
  check response has no minor input handling violations
